## 5. Audio Codecs & Digital Signal Processing (DSP)

### 5.1 PCM16 vs. Telephony G.711 $mu$-law & A-law
The Realtime API supports multiple audio encoding formats tailored to different deployment environments:

<div class="diagram-container">
  <img src="../assets/svg/diag_ch05_audio_codec_transcoding.svg" alt="Audio Codec Transcoding: PCM16 vs G.711 μ-law / A-law Pipeline" />
  <p class="diagram-caption">Figure 5.4: Audio Codec Ingestion Pipeline: Resampling, Companding &amp; Byte Packing Topology</p>
</div>

| Audio Codec | Sample Rate | Bit Depth | Bandwidth | Optimal Deployment Target |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Linear PCM 16-bit** (`pcm16`) | 24,000 Hz (24kHz) | 16-bit Signed LE | 384 kbps | Web Browsers, iOS/Android Apps, High-Fidelity Audio |
| **G.711 $mu$-law** (`g711_ulaw`) | 8,000 Hz (8kHz) | 8-bit Companded | 64 kbps | Telephony PSTN, Twilio Media Streams (North America) |
| **G.711 A-law** (`g711_alaw`) | 8,000 Hz (8kHz) | 8-bit Companded | 64 kbps | Telephony PSTN, SIP Trunks (Europe / International) |

### 5.2 The Mathematics of G.711 $mu$-law Companding
In telephony systems, transmitting 16-bit linear PCM audio over 64 kbps ISDN/PSTN channels was physically impossible. Engineers developed **Companding (Compressing-Expanding)** algorithms to squeeze 16-bit dynamic range into 8-bit bytes while preserving human speech intelligibility.

The $mu$-law companding transformation scales normalized input sample $x \in [-1, 1]$ according to logarithmic curve:

$$F(x) = \operatorname{sgn}(x) \frac{\ln(1 + \mu |x|)}{\ln(1 + \mu)}, \quad \mu = 255$$

By dedicating higher quantization resolution to low-amplitude vocal sounds while compressing loud peaks, $mu$-law achieves 12-bit perceptual audio quality inside an 8-bit byte.

When building telephony bridges (such as bridging Twilio phone calls to the Realtime API), developers configure `input_audio_format: "g711_ulaw"` and `output_audio_format: "g711_ulaw"`, enabling raw 8kHz byte payloads from Twilio WebSockets to pass directly to OpenAI without requiring computationally expensive server-side resampling!

